 ## Current release boundary
 
-Revision 102 completes `CAMPAIGN-PERF-QUAL1` as an integration/measurement gate without changing scientific or runtime algorithm authority. On a common 8,192-candidate/six-family target-data chain, the accumulated CPU optimization sequence reduces end-to-end FEAS1 -> MVIDX1 -> MVSEL1 -> REPAIR1 -> MVQUAL1 wall time from about 27.26 s in the PERFBASE1-era 0.20.225a0 control to a 4-lane median of about 11.95 s (~2.28x) while preserving exact reference, feasibility, sparse-index, selection, repair, and qualification digests. Replay indexed restart and EVAL2/bootstrap reductions reproduce their frozen exact records, and representative memory remains within the declared campaign ceiling. The reprofile also establishes that the dominant cost has shifted: MVSEL exact sparse state mutation and, especially, REPAIR replay of the already-computed selector state now dominate the target-data tail. Because REPAIR reconstructs essentially the complete selected-order state before proposal work, the CPU optimization program is not declared closed. `MVSTATE-REUSE1` is next to authenticate and hand terminal selector sparse state into REPAIR without changing selection or repair authority. Final GPU qualification remains deferred.
+Revision 103 completes `MVSTATE-REUSE1` and closes the exact-equivalence CPU optimization program. MVSEL now emits authenticated exact sparse-state checkpoints at materializable target rungs; REPAIR consumes those checkpoints only while its state is still identical to MVSEL and falls back to the historical carried-forward arithmetic after the first accepted repair swap. Pure checkpoint reconciliation after repair divergence was rejected because it perturbed FP64 representative-gain arrays at the 1e-17--1e-16 level. On the common 8,192-candidate/six-family closure fixture, untouched 0.20.235a0 takes about 12.00 s while 0.20.236a0 takes about 11.02 s excluding persistence; including the one-time ~0.18 s authenticated cache write, the fresh chain is about 11.19 s. REPAIR itself improves from about 5.37 s to 4.27 s with exact selection/repair/qualification digests. Cumulative fresh-chain speedup versus the PERFBASE1-era 0.20.225a0 authority is about 2.44x. Remaining target-chain cost is dominated by the exact sequential sparse-state arithmetic itself, so no further CPU-only gate is justified under the exact-equivalence policy. `FINAL-GPU1` is next; positive accelerator qualification remains deferred to that workstation gate.
 
-Revision 101 completes `REPLAY-PERF1` without changing REPLAY-UNIFY1 scientific authority. The immutable selected replay ExtXYZ now has a reconstructible source-SHA-bound byte-offset/natoms index under the internal replay cache. Indexed materialization seeks only requested source frames, reuses the authenticated source-order geometry identities instead of recomputing them after every parse, and feeds the existing true-label, pseudo-label, and foundation-prediction paths through deterministic bounded contiguous chunks. ASE parsing itself remains serial because qualification showed Python-threaded ExtXYZ parsing is slower on the available CPU. On the supplied 12,000-frame replay corpus, monitor-only true-label reconstruction improves from about 9.14 s to 3.01 s (~3.03x) with byte-identical output; a full-source parse/identity pass improves from about 7.64 s to 6.42 s (~1.19x), and full train+monitor materialization improves more modestly from about 15.68 s to 14.35 s (~1.09x) because all frames must still be parsed and written. The active qualification checkpoint remains MACE-MPA-0 medium, while the replay index/materialization contract is foundation-model independent and valid for MACE-MH-1. `CAMPAIGN-PERF-QUAL1` is next.
-
-Revision 100 completes `AUDIT-EVAL-PERF1` without changing model inference or GPU numerical authority. EVAL2 now caches immutable species/composition/focus/block indexing metadata per evaluation view, preallocates force-tail storage, and uses a memory-bounded batched paired bootstrap while preserving the deterministic RNG stream and exact persisted metric/bootstrap records on qualification fixtures. FOUNDATION-AUDIT1 now shares its DATA3 frame index and per-run species membership across domains, reuses force-square work, and batches tail quantiles while continuing to consume the already-authenticated DATA6 prediction sweep with zero additional model-provider calls. Same-host paired evidence shows about 1.92x faster repeated EVAL2 target reduction, 3.38x faster 2,000-replicate paired bootstrap, and a smaller ~1.06x foundation-audit CPU reduction on the available no-inference fixture. The active qualification checkpoint remains MACE-MPA-0 medium, while these CPU reducers remain foundation-model independent and valid for MACE-MH-1 campaigns. `REPLAY-PERF1` is next; GPU performance authority remains deferred.
